# Line endings, declared per path rather than globally.
#
# The point of the eol=lf rules is that a checkout of the sources is LF on every
# platform. A default Git for Windows install, and the GitHub Windows runners,
# check text out with core.autocrlf=true; this clone does not. That divergence
# is invisible until something compares generated output against the tree, which
# then passes locally and fails in CI (dbcodegen's changed-file check did
# exactly that). With these rules both see the bytes that are committed.
#
# There is deliberately no "* text=auto" line. A global rule would also claim
# the 124k game data records and the UTF-16LE localised text, where the bytes
# are written by the editor and the database tools and are not this file's
# business; leaving those paths out keeps git's own content detection in charge
# of them, which is what has been in charge of them until now.

# The engine sources and the resource scripts beside them. All 12 .rc are plain
# ASCII; none is UTF-16, so none needs an exception.
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.c      text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.cc     text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.cpp    text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.cxx    text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.h      text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.hpp    text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.hxx    text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.inl    text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.rc     text eol=lf
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/**/*.rc2    text eol=lf

# The .cll type descriptions dbcodegen reads. Their content is not touched by
# the hygiene hooks, because a tooltip comment reaches types.xml verbatim, but
# they still have to read the same on both platforms.
*.cll                                         text eol=lf

# The build system, the CI definitions, the scripts and the prose.
CMakeLists.txt                                text eol=lf
*.cmake                                       text eol=lf
CMakePresets.json                             text eol=lf
.github/**/*.yml                              text eol=lf
.github/**/*.yaml                             text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.py                               text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.sh                               text eol=lf
*.md                                          text eol=lf

# The parser's fixture corpus: every byte is input to NLang::Parse, including
# the mixed endings and the missing final newlines. -text means git converts
# nothing in either direction, so every platform parses what was committed. The
# !eol goes with it: eol only takes effect on a path git is treating as text,
# and leaving it set behind a -text is a trap for the next reader.
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/Parser/test/cases/** -text !eol

# Third-party drops, kept comparable with upstream. The pattern with a space in
# it has to be quoted, or git reads the second word as an attribute name.
Versions/Temporary/Engine/Sources/vendor/**   -text !eol
"Soft/SDK/STL docs/**"                        -text !eol
